Chapter: Deflate
  cites Compress chapter Lz77

 Deflate (RFC 1951). Stored blocks (BTYPE=00), fixed-Huffman compressed blocks
 (BTYPE=01), dynamic-Huffman compressed blocks (BTYPE=02), a chooser that picks
 the block type per block rather than once for the whole stream, and a decoder
 for all of them.

 This chapter used to emit stored blocks only. That was correct RFC 1951 and it
 was not compression: a stored block is the input verbatim behind a five-byte
 header, so deflate-compress returned MORE bytes than it was given, and every
 caller reaching for Gzip -- the de facto name for "make this smaller" -- got a
 valid gzip stream 23 bytes larger than its input. Nothing caught it for the
 same reason nothing ever catches a pass-through: the tests asserted a
 round-trip, and a pipe round-trips perfectly. A ROUND-TRIP TEST CANNOT TELL A
 COMPRESSOR FROM A PIPE. The tests here now assert the output is SMALLER, which
 is the one assertion a stored-block implementation cannot pass.

 What compression is, in this format: repeated byte runs become back-references,
 and the resulting symbols are entropy-coded. Both halves are needed. Literals
 alone under the fixed code cost 8 bits each for 0-143 -- no gain whatsoever over
 the input -- so it is the back-references that pay and the fixed code that makes
 them cheap.

 The matcher is Lz77's, and it now reaches exactly as far as RFC 1951 allows: a
 32768-byte window and matches up to 258. It used to stop at 255 and 255, which
 was never a format limit but the limit of a brute-force scan; Lz77 indexes
 positions by a hash chain now, so the window costs nothing to widen. Borrowing a
 matcher we already have and already test beats writing a second one.

 A match may reach back past the start of the block it lands in. That is ordinary
 RFC 1951 -- the window spans blocks, the decoder has the earlier output, and the
 per-block chooser below cuts blocks on the token stream for exactly that reason.

 BTYPE=02 (dynamic Huffman, where the code table is built from the data and
 shipped with the block) closes most of the remaining ratio gap against zlib on
 skewed data. It is deflate-compress-dynamic, below.

 What the fixed code cannot do is decline. It spends nine bits on every byte
 144..255, so high-byte or random input comes out LARGER than it went in.
 deflate-compress-best runs all three encoders and keeps the shortest, so the
 worst case is the stored block -- the input plus a five-byte header -- and
 never the fixed code's growth. A caller that means "make this smaller, and
 never larger" wants deflate-compress-best.

 It chooses per BLOCK, not once per stream. A stream is a sequence of blocks and
 each carries its own BTYPE, so an input that changes character part-way through
 -- text then noise, prose then compressed data -- does not have to pay one
 compromise across both halves. The whole-stream candidates are still built and
 still compete, so the per-block stream can win but never lose.

 To deflate-compress gives RFC 1951 fixed-Huffman compressed bytes.
 To deflate-compress-dynamic gives RFC 1951 dynamic-Huffman compressed bytes,
 with a code table built from the data's own frequencies.
 To deflate-compress-stored gives RFC 1951 stored-block bytes, which compress
 nothing and are the honest answer for data that does not compress.
 To deflate-compress-best gives the smallest stream it can build, choosing a
 block type per block.
 To deflate-decompress gives the original bytes from any block type.

 We say:

Section: Constants

  deflate-max-block : Integer = 65535
  deflate-end-of-block : Integer = 256

Section: The Bit Writer

 RFC 1951 section 3.1.1, and getting this backwards is the classic way to emit a
 stream that looks right and decodes to noise: everything EXCEPT a Huffman code
 is packed least-significant-bit first, and a Huffman code is packed
 most-significant-bit first. So the block header and every extra-bits field go
 through bw-bits, and every code goes through bw-code.

  BitWriter = record {
    bw-bytes : List Integer,
    bw-cur : Integer,
    bw-nbits : Integer
  }

  bw-empty : BitWriter
  bw-empty = BitWriter { bw-bytes = [], bw-cur = 0, bw-nbits = 0 }

  bw-bit : BitWriter, Integer -> BitWriter
  bw-bit (w) (b) =
    let cur = bit-or (w.bw-cur) (bit-shl (bit-and b 1) (w.bw-nbits))
    in if w.bw-nbits == 7
      then BitWriter { bw-bytes = list-push (w.bw-bytes) cur, bw-cur = 0, bw-nbits = 0 }
      else BitWriter { bw-bytes = w.bw-bytes, bw-cur = cur, bw-nbits = w.bw-nbits + 1 }

  bw-bits : BitWriter, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  bw-bits (w) (val) (n) = bw-bits-loop w val n 0

  bw-bits-loop : BitWriter, Integer, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  bw-bits-loop (w) (val) (n) (i) =
    if i >= n then w
    else bw-bits-loop (bw-bit w (bit-and (bit-shru val i) 1)) val n (i + 1)

  bw-code : BitWriter, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  bw-code (w) (code) (n) = bw-code-loop w code n (n - 1)

  bw-code-loop : BitWriter, Integer, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  bw-code-loop (w) (code) (n) (i) =
    if i < 0 then w
    else bw-code-loop (bw-bit w (bit-and (bit-shru code i) 1)) code n (i - 1)

  bw-finish : BitWriter -> List Integer
  bw-finish (w) =
    if w.bw-nbits == 0 then w.bw-bytes else list-push (w.bw-bytes) (w.bw-cur)

 A stored block starts on a byte boundary, so mid-stream it has to discard the
 rest of the byte it lands in. That is RFC 1951's "skip any remaining bits", and
 it is the only place the writer pads.

  bw-align : BitWriter -> BitWriter
  bw-align (w) =
    if w.bw-nbits == 0 then w
    else BitWriter { bw-bytes = list-push (w.bw-bytes) (w.bw-cur), bw-cur = 0, bw-nbits = 0 }

 Choosing a block type means measuring three encodings of the same tokens, and
 the measurement is the encoding: bw-pos counts the bits a writer holds, so a
 candidate costs exactly what it writes and no separate size model can drift
 away from the emitter.

 The three candidates are not written onto the real stream. bw-fork makes a
 writer that keeps only the bit PHASE -- the partial byte in flight -- and starts
 with no bytes, so each candidate holds one block rather than a copy of the whole
 output so far. bw-join then appends the winner. This is what keeps the memory
 bounded by the block and not by the stream: three candidates over a long input
 would otherwise retain three copies of everything already emitted.

 The join is exact because the candidate began in the same phase: its first
 completed byte IS the byte the real writer was part-way through, so appending
 its bytes and taking its phase reproduces the stream bit for bit.

  bw-pos : BitWriter -> Integer
  bw-pos (w) = list-length (w.bw-bytes) * 8 + w.bw-nbits

  bw-fork : BitWriter -> BitWriter
  bw-fork (w) = BitWriter { bw-bytes = [], bw-cur = w.bw-cur, bw-nbits = w.bw-nbits }

  bw-join : BitWriter, BitWriter -> BitWriter
  bw-join (w) (part) = BitWriter {
    bw-bytes = w.bw-bytes & part.bw-bytes,
    bw-cur = part.bw-cur,
    bw-nbits = part.bw-nbits
  }

Section: The Fixed Code

 Section 3.2.6's table as two functions. The boundaries are not decoration:
 0-143 cost 8 bits and 144-255 cost NINE, so a stream of high bytes under the
 fixed code is larger than its input. That is the format's bet that most data is
 mostly low bytes.

  fixed-lit-bits : Integer -> Integer
  fixed-lit-bits (sym) =
    if sym <= 143 then 8
    else if sym <= 255 then 9
    else if sym <= 279 then 7
    else 8

  fixed-lit-code : Integer -> Integer
  fixed-lit-code (sym) =
    if sym <= 143 then 48 + sym
    else if sym <= 255 then 400 + (sym - 144)
    else if sym <= 279 then sym - 256
    else 192 + (sym - 280)

Section: Length And Distance Codes

 Section 3.2.5. A match length of 3..258 becomes a symbol 257..285 plus extra
 bits; a distance of 1..32768 becomes a symbol 0..29 plus extra bits. The
 encoder and the decoder share these base and extra functions, so the two
 directions cannot disagree about the table.

  len-extra : Integer -> Integer
  len-extra (c) =
    if c <= 264 then 0
    else if c == 285 then 0
    else (c - 261) / 4

  len-base : Integer -> Integer
  len-base (c) =
    if c <= 264 then c - 254
    else if c == 285 then 258
    else if c <= 268 then 11 + (c - 265) * 2
    else if c <= 272 then 19 + (c - 269) * 4
    else if c <= 276 then 35 + (c - 273) * 8
    else if c <= 280 then 67 + (c - 277) * 16
    else 131 + (c - 281) * 32

  len-code-for : Integer -> Integer
  len-code-for (l) =
    if l <= 10 then 257 + (l - 3)
    else if l == 258 then 285
    else if l <= 18 then 265 + (l - 11) / 2
    else if l <= 34 then 269 + (l - 19) / 4
    else if l <= 66 then 273 + (l - 35) / 8
    else if l <= 130 then 277 + (l - 67) / 16
    else 281 + (l - 131) / 32

  dist-extra : Integer -> Integer
  dist-extra (c) = if c < 4 then 0 else (c - 2) / 2

  dist-base : Integer -> Integer
  dist-base (c) =
    if c < 4 then c + 1
    else let e = dist-extra c
    in bit-shl 1 (e + 1) + 1 + (c - (c / 2) * 2) * bit-shl 1 e

  dist-code-for : Integer -> Integer
  dist-code-for (d) =
    if d <= 4 then d - 1
    else if d <= 8 then 4 + (d - 5) / 2
    else if d <= 16 then 6 + (d - 9) / 4
    else if d <= 32 then 8 + (d - 17) / 8
    else if d <= 64 then 10 + (d - 33) / 16
    else if d <= 128 then 12 + (d - 65) / 32
    else if d <= 256 then 14 + (d - 129) / 64
    else if d <= 512 then 16 + (d - 257) / 128
    else if d <= 1024 then 18 + (d - 513) / 256
    else if d <= 2048 then 20 + (d - 1025) / 512
    else if d <= 4096 then 22 + (d - 2049) / 1024
    else if d <= 8192 then 24 + (d - 4097) / 2048
    else if d <= 16384 then 26 + (d - 8193) / 4096
    else 28 + (d - 16385) / 8192

Section: Compression (Fixed Huffman)

 A fixed-Huffman block has no length cap -- only a stored block does -- so a
 whole input fits in one, and deflate-compress emits exactly that.

 The block itself is written by dfl-fixed-block, which takes a writer and a
 BFINAL flag and gives a writer back rather than finishing a stream. All three
 encoders are shaped that way, because per-block type selection needs to put
 blocks of different types into ONE bit stream: a fixed or dynamic block does not
 end on a byte boundary, so a chooser cannot concatenate three finished streams,
 it has to keep writing into the same one.

 The header is three bits: BFINAL, then BTYPE=01, both least-significant-bit
 first, which puts 0b011 in the low bits of the first byte of a final block.

 It emits from TOKENS rather than driving the matcher itself. The token sequence
 is what dh-tokenize already produces for the dynamic path, so tokenizing once
 and encoding it three ways replaces three separate matcher runs over the same
 input.

  deflate-compress : List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-compress (input) =
    let toks = dh-tokenize input 0 (list-length input) []
    in bw-finish (dfl-fixed-block bw-empty toks (list-length toks) 1)

  dfl-fixed-block : BitWriter, List DhTok, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dfl-fixed-block (w) (toks) (nt) (bfinal) =
    let w0 = bw-bits (bw-bits w bfinal 1) 1 2
    in let w1 = dfl-fixed-toks w0 toks 0 nt
    in bw-code w1 (fixed-lit-code deflate-end-of-block) (fixed-lit-bits deflate-end-of-block)

  dfl-fixed-toks : BitWriter, List DhTok, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dfl-fixed-toks (w) (toks) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then w
    else when list-at toks i
      is DhLit (b) -> dfl-fixed-toks (deflate-emit-literal w b) toks (i + 1) n
      is DhMat (l) (d) -> dfl-fixed-toks (deflate-emit-match w l d) toks (i + 1) n

  deflate-emit-literal : BitWriter, Integer -> BitWriter
  deflate-emit-literal (w) (b) = bw-code w (fixed-lit-code b) (fixed-lit-bits b)

 A match is four fields in order: the length symbol, its extra bits, the
 distance symbol, its extra bits. The distance symbol is a five-bit Huffman code
 and goes most-significant-bit first like any other code; the extra bits do not.

  deflate-emit-match : BitWriter, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  deflate-emit-match (w) (mlen) (dist) =
    let lc = len-code-for mlen
    in let w1 = bw-code w (fixed-lit-code lc) (fixed-lit-bits lc)
    in let w2 = bw-bits w1 (mlen - len-base lc) (len-extra lc)
    in let dc = dist-code-for dist
    in let w3 = bw-code w2 dc 5
    in bw-bits w3 (dist - dist-base dc) (dist-extra dc)

Section: Dynamic Huffman -- Canonical Codes

 RFC 1951 section 3.2.2. A dynamic block ships its own code table, built from the
 data's own symbol frequencies, so the decoder rebuilds the exact codes from the
 code LENGTHS alone. Everything here works on a lengths array indexed by symbol
 (0 = symbol unused). The codes are canonical: symbols are ordered by (length,
 then symbol), and codes are assigned in increasing numeric order within each
 length. Encoder and decoder derive the same assignment from the same lengths.

  DhNode =
    | DhLeaf (Integer) (Integer)
    | DhBranch (Integer) (DhNode) (DhNode)

  dh-node-freq : DhNode -> Integer
  dh-node-freq (n) = when n
    is DhLeaf (s) (f) -> f
    is DhBranch (f) (l) (r) -> f

  dh-zeros : Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-zeros (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dh-zeros n (i + 1) (list-push acc 0)

  dh-leaves : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List DhNode -> List DhNode
  dh-leaves (freq) (i) (nsym) (acc) =
    if i >= nsym then acc
    else if list-at freq i > 0 then dh-leaves freq (i + 1) nsym (list-push acc (DhLeaf i (list-at freq i)))
    else dh-leaves freq (i + 1) nsym acc

  dh-find-min : List DhNode, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dh-find-min (nodes) (i) (len) (best) =
    if i >= len then best
    else if dh-node-freq (list-at nodes i) < dh-node-freq (list-at nodes best) then dh-find-min nodes (i + 1) len i
    else dh-find-min nodes (i + 1) len best

  dh-remove-at : List DhNode, Integer, Integer, Integer, List DhNode -> List DhNode
  dh-remove-at (nodes) (skip) (len) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else if i == skip then dh-remove-at nodes skip len (i + 1) acc
    else dh-remove-at nodes skip len (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at nodes i))

  dh-merge : List DhNode, Integer -> DhNode
  dh-merge (nodes) (count) =
    if count <= 1 then list-at nodes 0
    else let i1 = dh-find-min nodes 0 count 0
    in let n1 = list-at nodes i1
    in let r1 = dh-remove-at nodes i1 count 0 []
    in let i2 = dh-find-min r1 0 (count - 1) 0
    in let n2 = list-at r1 i2
    in let r2 = dh-remove-at r1 i2 (count - 1) 0 []
    in dh-merge (list-push r2 (DhBranch (dh-node-freq n1 + dh-node-freq n2) n1 n2)) (count - 1)

  dh-walk : DhNode, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-walk (node) (depth) (lengths) = when node
    is DhLeaf (s) (f) -> list-set-at lengths s (if depth == 0 then 1 else depth)
    is DhBranch (f) (l) (r) -> dh-walk r (depth + 1) (dh-walk l (depth + 1) lengths)

  dh-lengths-from-freq : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-lengths-from-freq (freq) (nsym) =
    let leaves = dh-leaves freq 0 nsym []
    in let count = list-length leaves
    in if count == 0 then dh-zeros nsym 0 []
    else dh-walk (dh-merge leaves count) 0 (dh-zeros nsym 0 [])

  dh-max-len : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dh-max-len (lengths) (i) (n) (best) =
    if i >= n then best
    else let v = list-at lengths i
    in dh-max-len lengths (i + 1) n (if v > best then v else best)

  dh-bl-count : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-bl-count (lengths) (nsym) =
    let maxlen = dh-max-len lengths 0 nsym 0
    in dh-bl-count-loop lengths 0 nsym (dh-zeros (maxlen + 1) 0 [])

  dh-bl-count-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-bl-count-loop (lengths) (i) (nsym) (acc) =
    if i >= nsym then acc
    else let l = list-at lengths i
    in if l > 0 then dh-bl-count-loop lengths (i + 1) nsym (list-set-at acc l (list-at acc l + 1))
    else dh-bl-count-loop lengths (i + 1) nsym acc

  dh-next-code : List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-next-code (bl) =
    let maxlen = list-length bl - 1
    in dh-next-code-loop bl 1 maxlen 0 (dh-zeros (maxlen + 1) 0 [])

  dh-next-code-loop : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-next-code-loop (bl) (len) (maxlen) (code) (acc) =
    if len > maxlen then acc
    else let code2 = bit-shl (code + list-at bl (len - 1)) 1
    in dh-next-code-loop bl (len + 1) maxlen code2 (list-set-at acc len code2)

  dh-canon-codes : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-canon-codes (lengths) (nsym) =
    let bl = dh-bl-count lengths nsym
    in let nc = dh-next-code bl
    in dh-assign-codes lengths 0 nsym nc (dh-zeros nsym 0 [])

  dh-assign-codes : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-assign-codes (lengths) (i) (nsym) (nc) (codes) =
    if i >= nsym then codes
    else let l = list-at lengths i
    in if l > 0 then
         let c = list-at nc l
         in dh-assign-codes lengths (i + 1) nsym (list-set-at nc l (c + 1)) (list-set-at codes i c)
       else dh-assign-codes lengths (i + 1) nsym nc codes

  dh-sorted-syms : List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-sorted-syms (lengths) (nsym) =
    let maxlen = dh-max-len lengths 0 nsym 0
    in dh-sorted-outer lengths nsym maxlen 1 []

  dh-sorted-outer : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-sorted-outer (lengths) (nsym) (maxlen) (len) (acc) =
    if len > maxlen then acc
    else dh-sorted-outer lengths nsym maxlen (len + 1) (dh-sorted-inner lengths nsym len 0 acc)

  dh-sorted-inner : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-sorted-inner (lengths) (nsym) (len) (sym) (acc) =
    if sym >= nsym then acc
    else if list-at lengths sym == len then dh-sorted-inner lengths nsym len (sym + 1) (list-push acc sym)
    else dh-sorted-inner lengths nsym len (sym + 1) acc

  DhTable = record {
    t-bl : List Integer,
    t-syms : List Integer,
    t-maxlen : Integer
  }

  dh-table-of : List Integer, Integer -> DhTable
  dh-table-of (lengths) (nsym) = DhTable {
    t-bl = dh-bl-count lengths nsym,
    t-syms = dh-sorted-syms lengths nsym,
    t-maxlen = dh-max-len lengths 0 nsym 0
  }

  DhSymRead = record {
    dr-sym : Integer,
    dr-reader : BitReader
  }

  dh-decode-sym : BitReader, DhTable -> DhSymRead
  dh-decode-sym (r) (t) = dh-decode-loop r (t.t-bl) (t.t-syms) (t.t-maxlen) 1 0 0 0

  dh-decode-loop : BitReader, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> DhSymRead
  dh-decode-loop (r) (bl) (syms) (maxlen) (len) (code) (first) (index) =
    if len > maxlen then DhSymRead { dr-sym = 0, dr-reader = r }
    else let code2 = bit-or code (br-bit r)
    in let r2 = br-advance r 1
    in let count = list-at bl len
    in if code2 - count < first then DhSymRead { dr-sym = list-at syms (index + (code2 - first)), dr-reader = r2 }
    else dh-decode-loop r2 bl syms maxlen (len + 1) (bit-shl code2 1) (bit-shl (first + count) 1) (index + count)

Section: Dynamic Huffman -- Compression

 One dynamic block for the whole input. Two passes: tokenize with the Lz77
 matcher and count symbol frequencies, then build the canonical codes and emit.
 If any code length exceeds the RFC limit (15 for literals and distances, 7 for
 the code-length alphabet) the block falls back to fixed Huffman, which is always
 valid -- length-limiting is not implemented, so we decline the block rather than
 emit an illegal code.

  DhTok =
    | DhLit (Integer)
    | DhMat (Integer) (Integer)

 The matcher keeps a hash chain now, so tokenizing carries the two tables. They
 are built here rather than by the caller because they must not outlive one
 tokenization -- they are mutated in place, and a pair shared between two calls
 would offer positions from the wrong input. Callers see the same four arguments
 they always did.

  dh-tokenize : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List DhTok -> List DhTok
  dh-tokenize (input) (pos) (len) (acc) =
    dh-tokenize-go input pos len acc (lz77-new-table lz77-hash-size) (lz77-new-table lz77-window-size) 8 0 lz77-max-match

 Tokenizing a slice of a longer input, with the bytes BEFORE it available to match
 against. Deflate never needs this -- it tokenizes a whole input at once -- but
 Brotli cuts its input into meta-blocks and a copy distance there reaches back
 through everything already emitted, not just through the current meta-block. A
 matcher started fresh at each cut cannot see any of it, so the window is the
 meta-block whatever the header declares.

 Seeding walks the history inserting positions without emitting tokens, so the
 chains point at real earlier positions and the match search is unchanged. The
 seed is bounded by the matcher's own window, since a candidate further back than
 that is rejected anyway.

 The caller states its own longest match, because the two formats that read this
 tokenizer do not share one. Deflate passes RFC 1951's 258; Brotli passes its own,
 which is larger by five orders of magnitude and is most of why its copies were
 short.

  dh-tokenize-from : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List DhTok, Integer -> List DhTok
  dh-tokenize-from (input) (start) (pos) (len) (acc) (cap) =
    let h0 = lz77-new-table lz77-hash-size
    in let p0 = lz77-new-table lz77-window-size
    in let h1 = dh-seed-tables input start pos len h0 p0
    in dh-tokenize-go input pos len acc h1 p0 8 0 cap

  dh-seed-tables : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-seed-tables (input) (i) (pos) (len) (head) (prev) =
    if i >= pos then head
    else dh-seed-tables input (i + 1) pos len (lz77-insert input i len head prev) prev

  dh-tokenize-go : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List DhTok, List Integer, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> List DhTok
  dh-tokenize-go (input) (pos) (len) (acc) (head) (prev) (lit-bits) (chunk-end) (cap) =
    if pos >= len then acc
    else if pos >= chunk-end then
      dh-tokenize-go input pos len acc head prev (lz77-lit-bits-at input pos len) (pos + lz77-est-window) cap
    else when lz77-find-lazy input pos len head prev lit-bits cap
      is Lz77Match (off) (mlen) ->
        dh-tokenize-go input (pos + mlen) len (list-push acc (DhMat mlen off)) (lz77-insert-run input pos mlen len head prev) prev lit-bits chunk-end cap
      is Lz77Literal (b) ->
        dh-tokenize-go input (pos + 1) len (list-push acc (DhLit b)) (lz77-insert input pos len head prev) prev lit-bits chunk-end cap

  dh-count-litlen : List DhTok, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-count-litlen (toks) (i) (n) (freq) =
    if i >= n then freq
    else when list-at toks i
      is DhLit (b) -> dh-count-litlen toks (i + 1) n (list-set-at freq b (list-at freq b + 1))
      is DhMat (l) (d) -> let c = len-code-for l in dh-count-litlen toks (i + 1) n (list-set-at freq c (list-at freq c + 1))

  dh-count-dist : List DhTok, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-count-dist (toks) (i) (n) (freq) =
    if i >= n then freq
    else when list-at toks i
      is DhLit (b) -> dh-count-dist toks (i + 1) n freq
      is DhMat (l) (d) -> let c = dist-code-for d in dh-count-dist toks (i + 1) n (list-set-at freq c (list-at freq c + 1))

  dh-any-positive : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Boolean
  dh-any-positive (arr) (i) (n) =
    if i >= n then False
    else if list-at arr i > 0 then True
    else dh-any-positive arr (i + 1) n

  dh-highest : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dh-highest (lengths) (i) =
    if i < 0 then 0
    else if list-at lengths i > 0 then i
    else dh-highest lengths (i - 1)

  dh-num-used : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dh-num-used (lengths) (nsym) (minv) =
    let n = dh-highest lengths (nsym - 1) + 1
    in if n < minv then minv else n

  DhCl = record {
    dc-sym : Integer,
    dc-extra : Integer,
    dc-nbits : Integer
  }

  dh-run-eq : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dh-run-eq (seq) (i) (n) (v) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else if list-at seq i == v then dh-run-eq seq (i + 1) n v (acc + 1)
    else acc

  dh-append-range : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-append-range (arr) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else dh-append-range arr n (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at arr i))

  dh-cl-seq : List Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-cl-seq (litlen) (numLit) (dist) (numDist) =
    dh-append-range dist numDist 0 (dh-append-range litlen numLit 0 [])

  dh-rle-16 : Integer, Integer, List DhCl -> List DhCl
  dh-rle-16 (v) (rem) (acc) =
    if rem >= 3 then let cnt = if rem > 6 then 6 else rem in dh-rle-16 v (rem - cnt) (list-push acc (DhCl { dc-sym = 16, dc-extra = cnt - 3, dc-nbits = 2 }))
    else if rem > 0 then dh-rle-16 v (rem - 1) (list-push acc (DhCl { dc-sym = v, dc-extra = 0, dc-nbits = 0 }))
    else acc

  dh-rle : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List DhCl -> List DhCl
  dh-rle (seq) (i) (n) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else let v = list-at seq i
    in if v == 0 then
         let run = dh-run-eq seq i n 0 0
         in if run >= 11 then let cnt = if run > 138 then 138 else run in dh-rle seq (i + cnt) n (list-push acc (DhCl { dc-sym = 18, dc-extra = cnt - 11, dc-nbits = 7 }))
         else if run >= 3 then dh-rle seq (i + run) n (list-push acc (DhCl { dc-sym = 17, dc-extra = run - 3, dc-nbits = 3 }))
         else dh-rle seq (i + 1) n (list-push acc (DhCl { dc-sym = 0, dc-extra = 0, dc-nbits = 0 }))
       else
         let run = dh-run-eq seq i n v 0
         in let acc1 = list-push acc (DhCl { dc-sym = v, dc-extra = 0, dc-nbits = 0 })
         in dh-rle seq (i + run) n (dh-rle-16 v (run - 1) acc1)

  dh-count-cl : List DhCl, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  dh-count-cl (items) (i) (n) (freq) =
    if i >= n then freq
    else let it = list-at items i
    in dh-count-cl items (i + 1) n (list-set-at freq (it.dc-sym) (list-at freq (it.dc-sym) + 1))

  dh-cl-order : Integer -> Integer
  dh-cl-order (j) = list-at [16, 17, 18, 0, 8, 7, 9, 6, 10, 5, 11, 4, 12, 3, 13, 2, 14, 1, 15] j

  dh-num-cl : List Integer -> Integer
  dh-num-cl (cllen) = dh-num-cl-loop cllen 18

  dh-num-cl-loop : List Integer, Integer -> Integer
  dh-num-cl-loop (cllen) (j) =
    if j < 4 then 4
    else if list-at cllen (dh-cl-order j) > 0 then j + 1
    else dh-num-cl-loop cllen (j - 1)

  dh-emit-cl-lengths : BitWriter, List Integer, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dh-emit-cl-lengths (w) (cllen) (numCl) (j) =
    if j >= numCl then w
    else dh-emit-cl-lengths (bw-bits w (list-at cllen (dh-cl-order j)) 3) cllen numCl (j + 1)

  dh-emit-cl-data : BitWriter, List DhCl, Integer, Integer, List Integer, List Integer -> BitWriter
  dh-emit-cl-data (w) (items) (i) (n) (clcodes) (cllen) =
    if i >= n then w
    else let it = list-at items i
    in let w1 = bw-code w (list-at clcodes (it.dc-sym)) (list-at cllen (it.dc-sym))
    in let w2 = if it.dc-nbits > 0 then bw-bits w1 (it.dc-extra) (it.dc-nbits) else w1
    in dh-emit-cl-data w2 items (i + 1) n clcodes cllen

  DhEnc = record {
    e-codes : List Integer,
    e-lens : List Integer
  }

  dh-put : BitWriter, DhEnc, Integer -> BitWriter
  dh-put (w) (enc) (sym) = bw-code w (list-at (enc.e-codes) sym) (list-at (enc.e-lens) sym)

  dh-emit-tokens : BitWriter, List DhTok, Integer, Integer, DhEnc, DhEnc -> BitWriter
  dh-emit-tokens (w) (toks) (i) (n) (litenc) (distenc) =
    if i >= n then w
    else when list-at toks i
      is DhLit (b) -> dh-emit-tokens (dh-put w litenc b) toks (i + 1) n litenc distenc
      is DhMat (l) (dd) ->
        let lc = len-code-for l
        in let w1 = dh-put w litenc lc
        in let w2 = bw-bits w1 (l - len-base lc) (len-extra lc)
        in let dc = dist-code-for dd
        in let w3 = dh-put w2 distenc dc
        in let w4 = bw-bits w3 (dd - dist-base dc) (dist-extra dc)
        in dh-emit-tokens w4 toks (i + 1) n litenc distenc

 A dynamic block can DECLINE. When a code length exceeds the RFC limit the block
 is not emittable, and in block form there is no whole-input fixed stream to fall
 back to -- the caller may be part-way through a stream. So dfl-dyn-block signals
 refusal by giving back the writer it was handed, untouched. A block that is
 emitted always writes at least its three header bits, so "the writer did not
 move" cannot be confused with a real block, and every caller tests for it before
 taking the result.

  deflate-compress-dynamic : List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-compress-dynamic (input) =
    let toks = dh-tokenize input 0 (list-length input) []
    in let w = dfl-dyn-block bw-empty toks (list-length toks) 1
    in if bw-pos w == 0 then deflate-compress input else bw-finish w

  dfl-dyn-block : BitWriter, List DhTok, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dfl-dyn-block (w) (toks) (nt) (bfinal) =
    let lf0 = dh-count-litlen toks 0 nt (dh-zeros 286 0 [])
    in let lf = list-set-at lf0 256 (list-at lf0 256 + 1)
    in let df0 = dh-count-dist toks 0 nt (dh-zeros 30 0 [])
    in let lllen = dh-lengths-from-freq lf 286
    in let dlen0 = dh-lengths-from-freq df0 30
    in let dlen = if dh-any-positive dlen0 0 30 then dlen0 else list-set-at dlen0 0 1
    in if dh-max-len lllen 0 286 0 > 15 then w
    else if dh-max-len dlen 0 30 0 > 15 then w
    else dh-emit-dynamic w toks nt lllen dlen bfinal

  dh-emit-dynamic : BitWriter, List DhTok, Integer, List Integer, List Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dh-emit-dynamic (w) (toks) (nt) (lllen) (dlen) (bfinal) =
    let numLit = dh-num-used lllen 286 257
    in let numDist = dh-num-used dlen 30 1
    in let seq = dh-cl-seq lllen numLit dlen numDist
    in let items = dh-rle seq 0 (list-length seq) []
    in let clf = dh-count-cl items 0 (list-length items) (dh-zeros 19 0 [])
    in let cllen = dh-lengths-from-freq clf 19
    in if dh-max-len cllen 0 19 0 > 7 then w
    else
      let litcodes = dh-canon-codes lllen 286
      in let distcodes = dh-canon-codes dlen 30
      in let clcodes = dh-canon-codes cllen 19
      in let numCl = dh-num-cl cllen
      in let w0 = bw-bits (bw-bits w bfinal 1) 2 2
      in let w1 = bw-bits w0 (numLit - 257) 5
      in let w2 = bw-bits w1 (numDist - 1) 5
      in let w3 = bw-bits w2 (numCl - 4) 4
      in let w4 = dh-emit-cl-lengths w3 cllen numCl 0
      in let w5 = dh-emit-cl-data w4 items 0 (list-length items) clcodes cllen
      in let litenc = DhEnc { e-codes = litcodes, e-lens = lllen }
      in let distenc = DhEnc { e-codes = distcodes, e-lens = dlen }
      in let w6 = dh-emit-tokens w5 toks 0 nt litenc distenc
      in dh-put w6 litenc deflate-end-of-block

Section: Compression (Stored Blocks)

  deflate-compress-stored : List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-compress-stored (input) =
    let len = list-length input
    in deflate-emit-blocks input len 0

  deflate-emit-blocks : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-emit-blocks (input) (total) (pos) =
    if pos >= total then []
    else let remaining = total - pos
    in let chunk = if remaining > deflate-max-block then deflate-max-block else remaining
    in let is-last = pos + chunk >= total
    in let header = deflate-stored-header is-last chunk
    in let data = deflate-slice input pos chunk 0 []
    in header & data & deflate-emit-blocks input total (pos + chunk)

 The same block written into a stream rather than onto a byte array. It aligns
 after the header, then puts LEN, NLEN and the bytes through the writer, so a
 stored block can sit between two compressed ones. The padding is charged to the
 candidate, which is right: mid-stream a stored block costs up to seven bits more
 than it does at the start, and the chooser should see that.

  dfl-stored-block : BitWriter, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dfl-stored-block (w) (input) (off) (len) (bfinal) =
    let w0 = bw-align (bw-bits (bw-bits w bfinal 1) 0 2)
    in let nlen = bit-xor len 65535
    in let w1 = bw-bits (bw-bits w0 (bit-and len 255) 8) (bit-and (bit-shru len 8) 255) 8
    in let w2 = bw-bits (bw-bits w1 (bit-and nlen 255) 8) (bit-and (bit-shru nlen 8) 255) 8
    in dfl-stored-bytes w2 input off len 0

  dfl-stored-bytes : BitWriter, List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer -> BitWriter
  dfl-stored-bytes (w) (input) (off) (len) (i) =
    if i >= len then w
    else dfl-stored-bytes (bw-bits w (list-at input (off + i)) 8) input off len (i + 1)

  deflate-stored-header : Boolean, Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-stored-header (is-last) (len) =
    let bfinal = if is-last then 1 else 0
    in let len-lo = bit-and len 255
    in let len-hi = bit-and (bit-shru len 8) 255
    in let nlen-lo = bit-and (bit-xor len 65535) 255
    in let nlen-hi = bit-and (bit-shru (bit-xor len 65535) 8) 255
    in [bfinal, len-lo, len-hi, nlen-lo, nlen-hi]

Section: Compression (Per-Block Choice)

 Each of the three encoders emits a complete, self-describing stream with BFINAL
 set, so deflate-decompress reads whichever one it is handed, and picking the
 shortest of the three is already never longer than the stored block. What that
 whole-stream pick cannot do is change its mind part-way through an input. One
 BTYPE has to cover everything, so a file that is half text and half compressed
 data or noise pays one compromise across both halves: choose dynamic and the
 noisy half inflates, choose stored and the text half is not compressed at all.

 RFC 1951 does not ask for that compromise. A stream is a SEQUENCE of blocks and
 every block carries its own BTYPE, so the text half can be dynamic and the noisy
 half stored, in one stream, with the type chosen where the data changes.

 That is what dfl-chunked does. It cuts the token stream into regions, and for
 each region writes all three block types into forked writers, keeps whichever
 came out shortest in BITS, and joins it. Because the candidates all start from
 the same bit phase the comparison is exact, and because the winner is a writer
 already holding the encoded block there is nothing to encode twice.

 The region is a target, not a boundary: a match cannot be split, so a region
 ends at the first token that reaches deflate-region bytes of output. Regions are
 cut on the tokens, never on the input, which is what keeps matches that reach
 back across a boundary intact -- the window spans blocks, and a back-reference
 into the previous block is ordinary RFC 1951.

 The size of a region trades table overhead against how finely the type can
 follow the data. Smaller regions track a change of character sooner; larger ones
 amortize the dynamic block's code table, which is the better part of a hundred
 bytes. This is why the chunked stream does not simply replace the whole-stream
 one: deflate-compress-best keeps BOTH and takes the shorter, so chunking can win
 but can never lose, and an input whose character never changes still gets the
 single block that suits it. Below one region the two are the same stream.

  deflate-region : Integer = 4096

  DflRegion = record {
    rg-tok-end : Integer,
    rg-bytes : Integer
  }

  dfl-tok-len : DhTok -> Integer
  dfl-tok-len (t) = when t
    is DhLit (b) -> 1
    is DhMat (l) (d) -> l

  dfl-region-end : List DhTok, Integer, Integer, Integer -> DflRegion
  dfl-region-end (toks) (i) (nt) (acc) =
    if i >= nt then DflRegion { rg-tok-end = i, rg-bytes = acc }
    else if acc >= deflate-region then DflRegion { rg-tok-end = i, rg-bytes = acc }
    else dfl-region-end toks (i + 1) nt (acc + dfl-tok-len (list-at toks i))

  dfl-tok-slice : List DhTok, Integer, Integer, List DhTok -> List DhTok
  dfl-tok-slice (toks) (i) (e) (acc) =
    if i >= e then acc
    else dfl-tok-slice toks (i + 1) e (list-push acc (list-at toks i))

  dfl-shorter-w : BitWriter, BitWriter -> BitWriter
  dfl-shorter-w (a) (b) = if bw-pos b < bw-pos a then b else a

  dfl-chunked : List Integer, List DhTok, Integer, Integer, Integer, BitWriter -> List Integer
  dfl-chunked (input) (toks) (ti) (nt) (off) (w) =
    let rg = dfl-region-end toks ti nt 0
    in let te = rg.rg-tok-end
    in let nbytes = rg.rg-bytes
    in let bfinal = if te >= nt then 1 else 0
    in let part = dfl-tok-slice toks ti te []
    in let np = list-length part
    in let base = bw-fork w
    in let cs = dfl-stored-block base input off nbytes bfinal
    in let cf = dfl-fixed-block base part np bfinal
    in let cd = dfl-dyn-block base part np bfinal
    in let best0 = dfl-shorter-w cs cf
    in let best = if bw-pos cd > bw-pos base then dfl-shorter-w best0 cd else best0
    in let w2 = bw-join w best
    in if bfinal == 1 then bw-finish w2
    else dfl-chunked input toks te nt (off + nbytes) w2

 One tokenization feeds all four candidates. The matcher used to run three times
 over the same input; now it runs once and the token sequence is encoded four
 ways, which is the cheaper half of this change.

  deflate-shorter : List Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-shorter (a) (b) =
    if list-length a <= list-length b then a else b

  deflate-compress-best : List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-compress-best (input) =
    let toks = dh-tokenize input 0 (list-length input) []
    in let nt = list-length toks
    in let fixed = bw-finish (dfl-fixed-block bw-empty toks nt 1)
    in let wd = dfl-dyn-block bw-empty toks nt 1
    in let dyn = if bw-pos wd == 0 then fixed else bw-finish wd
    in let whole = deflate-shorter (deflate-shorter (deflate-compress-stored input) fixed) dyn
    in deflate-shorter whole (dfl-chunked input toks 0 nt 0 bw-empty)

Section: The Bit Reader

  BitReader = record {
    br-bytes : List Integer,
    br-pos : Integer
  }

  br-bit : BitReader -> Integer
  br-bit (r) =
    let byte-i = (r.br-pos) / 8
    in if byte-i >= list-length (r.br-bytes) then 0
    else bit-and (bit-shru (list-at (r.br-bytes) byte-i) ((r.br-pos) - byte-i * 8)) 1

  br-advance : BitReader, Integer -> BitReader
  br-advance (r) (n) = BitReader { br-bytes = r.br-bytes, br-pos = (r.br-pos) + n }

  br-bits : BitReader, Integer -> Integer
  br-bits (r) (n) = br-bits-loop r n 0 0

  br-bits-loop : BitReader, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  br-bits-loop (r) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else br-bits-loop (br-advance r 1) n (i + 1) (bit-or acc (bit-shl (br-bit r) i))

  br-code : BitReader, Integer -> Integer
  br-code (r) (n) = br-code-loop r n 0 0

  br-code-loop : BitReader, Integer, Integer, Integer -> Integer
  br-code-loop (r) (n) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= n then acc
    else br-code-loop (br-advance r 1) n (i + 1) (bit-or (bit-shl acc 1) (br-bit r))

  br-align : BitReader -> BitReader
  br-align (r) =
    let rem = (r.br-pos) - ((r.br-pos) / 8) * 8
    in if rem == 0 then r else br-advance r (8 - rem)

Section: Decoding A Fixed Symbol

 The fixed code is decoded by width, shortest first: seven bits decides
 end-of-block and the low length symbols, eight decides the literals below 144
 and the high length symbols, nine decides the rest. Reading a bit at a time and
 widening is what makes a prefix code decodable without a table.

  fixed-sym : BitReader -> Integer
  fixed-sym (r) =
    let v7 = br-code r 7
    in if v7 <= 23 then 256 + v7
    else fixed-sym-8 r (bit-or (bit-shl v7 1) (br-bit (br-advance r 7)))

  fixed-sym-8 : BitReader, Integer -> Integer
  fixed-sym-8 (r) (v8) =
    if v8 <= 191 then v8 - 48
    else if v8 <= 199 then 280 + (v8 - 192)
    else 144 + ((bit-or (bit-shl v8 1) (br-bit (br-advance r 8))) - 400)

  fixed-sym-width : BitReader -> Integer
  fixed-sym-width (r) =
    let v7 = br-code r 7
    in if v7 <= 23 then 7
    else let v8 = bit-or (bit-shl v7 1) (br-bit (br-advance r 7))
    in if v8 <= 199 then 8 else 9

Section: Decompression

 Both block types, dispatched on BTYPE. A stored block realigns to the byte
 boundary and copies verbatim; a fixed block reads symbols until end-of-block.

  deflate-decompress : List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-decompress (input) =
    deflate-blocks (BitReader { br-bytes = input, br-pos = 0 }) []

  deflate-blocks : BitReader, List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-blocks (r) (acc) =
    if (r.br-pos) / 8 >= list-length (r.br-bytes) then acc
    else let bfinal = br-bit r
    in let btype = br-bits (br-advance r 1) 2
    in let r2 = br-advance r 3
    in if btype == 0 then deflate-after-stored (br-align r2) bfinal acc
    else if btype == 1 then deflate-after-fixed (deflate-fixed-block r2 acc) bfinal
    else if btype == 2 then deflate-after-fixed (deflate-dynamic-block r2 acc) bfinal
    else acc

 A stored block's LEN and NLEN sit on the byte boundary after the header bits.

  deflate-after-stored : BitReader, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-after-stored (r) (bfinal) (acc) =
    let off = (r.br-pos) / 8
    in let len = bit-or (list-at (r.br-bytes) off) (bit-shl (list-at (r.br-bytes) (off + 1)) 8)
    in let data = deflate-slice (r.br-bytes) (off + 4) len 0 []
    in let acc2 = acc & data
    in if bfinal == 1 then acc2
    else deflate-blocks (BitReader { br-bytes = r.br-bytes, br-pos = (off + 4 + len) * 8 }) acc2

  DeflateState = record {
    ds-reader : BitReader,
    ds-out : List Integer
  }

  deflate-fixed-block : BitReader, List Integer -> DeflateState
  deflate-fixed-block (r) (acc) =
    let sym = fixed-sym r
    in let r2 = br-advance r (fixed-sym-width r)
    in if sym == deflate-end-of-block then DeflateState { ds-reader = r2, ds-out = acc }
    else if sym < 256 then deflate-fixed-block r2 (list-push acc sym)
    else deflate-fixed-match r2 acc sym

  deflate-fixed-match : BitReader, List Integer, Integer -> DeflateState
  deflate-fixed-match (r) (acc) (sym) =
    let mlen = len-base sym + br-bits r (len-extra sym)
    in let r2 = br-advance r (len-extra sym)
    in let dc = br-code r2 5
    in let r3 = br-advance r2 5
    in let dist = dist-base dc + br-bits r3 (dist-extra dc)
    in let r4 = br-advance r3 (dist-extra dc)
    in deflate-fixed-block r4 (deflate-copy acc (list-length acc - dist) mlen)

  deflate-copy : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-copy (out) (src) (remaining) =
    if remaining <= 0 then out
    else deflate-copy (list-push out (list-at out src)) (src + 1) (remaining - 1)

  deflate-after-fixed : DeflateState, Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-after-fixed (st) (bfinal) =
    if bfinal == 1 then st.ds-out
    else deflate-blocks (st.ds-reader) (st.ds-out)

Section: Decoding A Dynamic Block

 The mirror of the dynamic encoder. Read HLIT/HDIST/HCLEN, read the code-length
 code lengths in their permuted order and build that table, decode the literal
 and distance code lengths through it (with the 16/17/18 run codes), build those
 two tables, then decode symbols exactly as the fixed path does but through the
 data's own codes.

  DhLenRead = record {
    dl-list : List Integer,
    dl-reader : BitReader
  }

  dh-read-cl-lengths : BitReader, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> DhLenRead
  dh-read-cl-lengths (r) (numCl) (acc) (j) =
    if j >= numCl then DhLenRead { dl-list = acc, dl-reader = r }
    else let v = br-bits r 3
    in dh-read-cl-lengths (br-advance r 3) numCl (list-set-at acc (dh-cl-order j) v) (j + 1)

  dh-repeat-append : List Integer, Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-repeat-append (acc) (val) (cnt) =
    if cnt <= 0 then acc
    else dh-repeat-append (list-push acc val) val (cnt - 1)

  dh-read-cls : BitReader, DhTable, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> DhLenRead
  dh-read-cls (r) (cltab) (need) (acc) (prev) =
    if list-length acc >= need then DhLenRead { dl-list = acc, dl-reader = r }
    else let d = dh-decode-sym r cltab
    in let sym = d.dr-sym
    in let r2 = d.dr-reader
    in if sym <= 15 then dh-read-cls r2 cltab need (list-push acc sym) sym
       else if sym == 16 then let cnt = 3 + br-bits r2 2 in dh-read-cls (br-advance r2 2) cltab need (dh-repeat-append acc prev cnt) prev
       else if sym == 17 then let cnt = 3 + br-bits r2 3 in dh-read-cls (br-advance r2 3) cltab need (dh-repeat-append acc 0 cnt) 0
       else let cnt = 11 + br-bits r2 7 in dh-read-cls (br-advance r2 7) cltab need (dh-repeat-append acc 0 cnt) 0

  dh-fill : List Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer, Integer -> List Integer
  dh-fill (src) (srcoff) (count) (dst) (i) =
    if i >= count then dst
    else dh-fill src srcoff count (list-set-at dst i (list-at src (srcoff + i))) (i + 1)

  deflate-dynamic-block : BitReader, List Integer -> DeflateState
  deflate-dynamic-block (r) (acc) =
    let numLit = 257 + br-bits r 5
    in let numDist = 1 + br-bits (br-advance r 5) 5
    in let numCl = 4 + br-bits (br-advance r 10) 4
    in let clread = dh-read-cl-lengths (br-advance r 14) numCl (dh-zeros 19 0 []) 0
    in let cltab = dh-table-of (clread.dl-list) 19
    in let allread = dh-read-cls (clread.dl-reader) cltab (numLit + numDist) [] 0
    in let alllen = allread.dl-list
    in let lllen = dh-fill alllen 0 numLit (dh-zeros 286 0 []) 0
    in let dlen = dh-fill alllen numLit numDist (dh-zeros 30 0 []) 0
    in deflate-dyn-loop (allread.dl-reader) acc (dh-table-of lllen 286) (dh-table-of dlen 30)

  deflate-dyn-loop : BitReader, List Integer, DhTable, DhTable -> DeflateState
  deflate-dyn-loop (r) (acc) (ll) (dt) =
    let d = dh-decode-sym r ll
    in let sym = d.dr-sym
    in let r2 = d.dr-reader
    in if sym == deflate-end-of-block then DeflateState { ds-reader = r2, ds-out = acc }
    else if sym < 256 then deflate-dyn-loop r2 (list-push acc sym) ll dt
    else let mlen = len-base sym + br-bits r2 (len-extra sym)
    in let r3 = br-advance r2 (len-extra sym)
    in let dd = dh-decode-sym r3 dt
    in let dsym = dd.dr-sym
    in let r4 = dd.dr-reader
    in let dist = dist-base dsym + br-bits r4 (dist-extra dsym)
    in let r5 = br-advance r4 (dist-extra dsym)
    in deflate-dyn-loop r5 (deflate-copy acc (list-length acc - dist) mlen) ll dt

Section: Helpers

  deflate-slice : List Integer, Integer, Integer, Integer, List Integer -> List Integer
  deflate-slice (xs) (off) (len) (i) (acc) =
    if i >= len then acc
    else if off + i >= list-length xs then acc
    else deflate-slice xs off len (i + 1) (list-push acc (list-at xs (off + i)))
